One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when performing file operations, which can lead to **Path Traversal** attacks. If an attacker provides input like `../../etc/passwd`, they may access sensitive files outside the intended directory.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class FileHandler {
    public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // This code is vulnerable to Path Traversal because it directly uses 
        // user-provided input to construct a file path without validation.
        File file = new File("/app/user_uploads/" + filename);
        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(file.toPath());
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
***
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class FileHandler {
    public void readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        // Define the base directory as an absolute path
        Path baseDirectory = Paths.get("/app/user_uploads/").toAbsolutePath().normalize();

        // Resolve the user input against the base directory and normalize the resulting path
        Path targetPath = baseDirectory.resolve(filename).normalize();

        // Verify that the resulting path still starts with the intended base directory
        if (!targetPath.startsWith(baseDirectory)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Invalid file path attempt: " + filename);
        }

        // The path is now safe to use
        byte[] content = Files.readAllBytes(targetPath);
        System.out.println(new String(content));
    }
}
```

